# k31rel. docs/cards/k31-room-sweep.card's 232G row (the plan flipped to a 229 GB arena, host segment 6.5 GB) read 1.0 GB a position
# over one 640-position request and was ruled worse than the static host set; but that request starts with an empty arena where the
# static set starts populated (250 GB warm), so the row's bytes hold the arena's fill (229 GB) plus its misses, not its steady state.
# The fadvise route meanwhile measures a page-cache fill ceiling near 1.7 GB/s whatever the issue order (docs/cards/k31-mirror-probe.card
# runs 2-6), so the O_DIRECT reader into an anonymous arena (no page cache, no rmap, no TLB shootdowns) is the candidate path, and its
# one unmeasured term is the warm arena's bytes a position. This probe is the 232G row with n_predict 1024 (the decode alone
# ~0.9 GB x 1024 at 3 tok/s: ~6 min), reading iostat's rMB/s and the token rate over the decode's course: a warming arena reads
# fewer bytes a position as it fills, and the last 128 positions' rate is the steady-state estimate. No code changes.
kind: calibration
question: over 1024 decode positions on the 229 GB arena plan, do bytes a position fall from ~1.0 GB toward the static set's 0.45-0.7, and what is the last quarter's decode rate?
term: decode tok/s @ D=512 over n_predict 1024 on the 0.3.13 binary at BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=232G (arena=229 GB), with iostat rMB/s per decode quarter beside it
unit: tok/s
predict: 2.8..4.5
condition: tools/ref/k31-nvme-probe.sh with K31_PROBE_N=1024 and K31_PROBE_ENV=BLOOMERY_HOST_ROOM=232G (the later env wins), the 0.3.13 sm86 tarball's
  bloomery-serve, --place bp --ctx-size 2048, the first 512 prose ids, under one lease; the plan line must print arena near
  229 GB (else the plan did not flip and the row is not read); the decode's iostat split into quarters of ~256 positions
reason: [derived] the room sweep's 232G row: 634 GB over 640 positions with the arena filling from empty; if the arena's victim rule
  keeps a hot set, the fill is ~229 GB of that and the misses ~0.6 GB a position early, falling toward the static set's ~0.5 as it
  fills; at the reader's 2.2 GB/s a 0.5 GB position is ~230 ms + ~40 = 3.7 tok/s in the last quarter. If the bytes do not fall,
  the arena's victim rule does not keep the hot set and the reader needs a real LRU before it is the K3.1 path
decide-in: the arena is the K3.1 decode path to build on: the mirror moves into the reader (two directories, the drive with fewer reads
  in flight per read), the reader's depth and read size are raised (2 MiB parts, both drives), and a populate of the arena at load
  from the rank file replaces the empty start
decide-below: under 2.8 or bytes flat near 1.0 GB a position: the arena's victim rule is the defect (read crates/gpu/src/host for the
  slot choice) and is fixed before any reader lever; the fadvise route stays the shipped path at 3.7
decide-above: above 4.5: the warm arena beats the static set already; the mirror-in-reader round is the next and the fadvise route
  is dropped
# outcome 2026-11-07 (one request, plan arena=228815781760 host_experts=76910 at 6.5 GB paged=502 GB, n_predict 1024, 139 major
#  faults, 984 GB over 1,536 positions): tg 3.80 / pp 2.57 tok/s, in the band. nvme0 per decode quarter (256 positions each):
#  q1 2,283 MB/s = 0.59 GB a position (the fill), q2 1,320 = 0.34, q3 1,299 = 0.33, q4 1,420 = 0.36; aqu-sz 4.6-8.9, rareq-sz
#  ~560 KiB, r_await 1.9-2.3 ms; nvme1 idle (the tier's reader knows no mirror). decide-in: the warm arena keeps the hot set better
#  than the populated static set (0.34 against the mapping's 0.45-0.6 GB a position), so the O_DIRECT reader is the K3.1 path to build
#  on. But the step did not fall with the bytes: [derived] 0.34 GB / 92 = 3.7 MB a layer = ~6-7 requests of ~560 KiB, in flight
#  together for ~2 ms (r_await), + DRAM 0.33 ms, x 92 = ~215-250 ms: the layer's batch latency, not the bytes, is the step, on
#  this route as on the mapping's. The 560 KiB requests are the block layer's cut of a ~2 MiB part read into 4 KiB-paged arena
#  memory (max_segments 128); the drive's latency at that size and depth is the next measurement (docs/cards/k31-fio-lat.card):
#  at ~0.5 ms a batch the route reaches [derived] 92 x 0.85 + 10 = ~90 ms = ~10 tok/s; at 2 ms it stays near 4.5 and the lever
#  is hiding the batch (layer-ahead prefetch, several tokens a step).
